/*
# DataGrid

Records as rows, one column per field you choose: headers that sort, reorder and resize by
drag (touch-correct), selection and keyboard travel from `Table`, and a windowed collection,
so ten thousand rows cost a screenful of views.

A DataGrid has **no children**. What it shows comes from two attributes: `items`, the
records, one row each; and `columns`, plain records, one column each.

```declare
App [ width = 440, height = 220,
    d: Dataset { { "rows": [ { "id": 1, "title": "Fix the rail",      "state": "open" },
                             { "id": 2, "title": "Draft the chapter", "state": "done" },
                             { "id": 3, "title": "Measure the grid",  "state": "open" } ] } },
    DataGrid [ x = 20, y = 50, width = 400, height = 130, selects = "multi",
        items = { app.d.value.rows },
        columns = { [ { id: "id",    title: "ID",    width: 50 },
                      { id: "title", title: "Title", width: 220 },
                      { id: "state", title: "State", width: 110 } ] } ]
    ]
```

Drag a header sideways to reorder; drag the divider between two headers to resize; click a
header to sort.

- **Items** are records, kept matched to their rows by each record's `id`. Taken from a
  `Dataset`, they are the dataset's own records: an edit made in a cell writes the record,
  and everything that reads it follows.
- **Columns** are records with an `id` (the field the column shows, and its name), a
  `title`, a `width`, and optionally a `cell` or `edit`. Being data, they can be built,
  stored, or chosen by the person, like any records.
- **A cell** shows its field as text, unless its column names a class: `cell: "DoneCell"`
  draws that class in every row of the column, each copy bound to its own row's record (so
  `:done` is that row's `done`). Cells in one column can differ by row: the class decides
  how to look for its record, with a `State` or a `classFor` inside it.
- **`edit: true`** makes a column's cells text fields that write the record as you type,
  with a spreadsheet's keys: Enter, F2 or a typed character starts the edit, Up and Down
  move it a row, Esc puts the text back.
- **A heading row** is a record with a `heading` field — `{ id: "g1", heading: "Overdue" }` —
  drawn across the full width, with no cells, passed over by selection and the arrow keys.
  `headingClass` names a class of your own to draw it.
- **Sorting names a derivation.** A header click sets `sortOn` and `sortDir`; your app
  derives the sorted items from them, and the grid shows the order it is given.
- **Order and widths** are the grid's state, `order` and `widths`, delivered through
  `arrangeInput` and `resizeInput`, so an app can persist a person's layout.

## items
The records, one row each, in the order shown. Usually a dataset's list —
`items = { app.tasks.value.rows }` — or one derived from it (sorted, filtered, grouped). A
record whose `heading` field is set is a heading row.

## columns
The columns, as records, in their first order: `id` (the field shown, and the column's
name), `title` (the header; the id when absent), `width` (in pixels; 120 when absent),
and optionally `cell` (the name of a class drawn in each of the column's cells) or `edit`
(`true` for text fields). **The column model follows this list**: add, remove or change a
record and the grid follows.

## headingClass
The name of the class that draws a heading row — `""` for the grid's own, the record's
`heading` text in the theme's muted ink. The class is placed across the row, with the
heading record as its data.

## order
The presented column order, as column ids — `null` means the order of `columns`. Part of
the grid's value-pattern state: delivered through `arrangeInput`, so an app that overrides
that owns the order and can persist it.

## widths
Per-column width overrides, `{ id: width }` — `null` means the widths in `columns`. **A
width of `0` drops the column entirely**, which is the deliberate narrow-screen degrade:
priority, not squish. `widths = { app.narrow ? { notes: 0 } : null }` is a responsive
layout.

## sortOn
The column id the sort *names* — `""` when unsorted. **Sorting names a derivation; it
does not reorder anything.** The truth stays in the data: your app derives the sorted items
from this, and the grid displays the order it is given.

## sortDir
`"asc"` or `"desc"`, alongside `sortOn`.

## sortInput()
**Delivery seam for sorting.** The default writes `sortOn`/`sortDir`; override it to own
the sort — which is what you do when the sort belongs to a server query.

## arrangeInput()
**Delivery seam for reordering**, receiving the new column-id order. Override it to
persist a user's column layout.

## resizeInput()
**Delivery seam for resizing**, receiving a column id and its new width. Widths are
plain values — **nothing measures cells** — so an override is free to clamp, snap, or
store them.

## headerClick()
Toggles the sort on a column: same column flips direction, a new column starts
ascending. Routes through `sortInput`.

## headerH
The header row's height.

## selectable()
`Table`'s, answered here: a heading record is not selectable, every other record is.
An override narrows it with `super.selectable(m)`, so headings stay out.

## _editStep()
**Internal.** Moves an edit to the same column one row up or down, passing over headings and scrolling the row into existence.

## _columnRecords()
**Internal.** The derived column model: the `columns` records ordered and widthed by state, with running x offsets.

## _currentOrder()
**Internal.** The column ids in their present order.

## _colWidthOf()
**Internal.** The present width of a column by id.

## _dragCol
**Internal.** The column id currently being dragged.

## _dropAt
**Internal.** The index the insertion bar marks, or −1.

## _dropIndexFor()
**Internal.** Which slot a dragged header's centre would land in.

## _dropBarX()
**Internal.** The insertion bar's x for a drop index.

## _commitDrop()
**Internal.** Commits a reorder at release — drop-commit, since swapping under the
pointer re-derives the spans mid-drag and flickers.

## _prevColId()
**Internal.** The column left of a given one, for the resize grip.

## _isDataGrid
**Internal.** The marker rows and headers guard on — not API.

## _firstRow()
**Internal.** The index of the first row that can be stood on, or −1.

## inHeader
Whether the keyboard position is in the header row: Up from the first row goes there,
Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the column, Down comes back.

## activeCol
The active cell's column — with Table's active row, the grid's keyboard position. Left and Right move it.

## _activeCell()
**Internal.** The active cell, when its row is built.

## _enterCell()
**Internal.** Enters a cell: an edit cell's field takes the focus, else its first control does, or for Space is pressed.

## onKeyDown
Left and Right move the active cell; Enter, F2 or a typed character edits a text column;
Enter or F2 moves into a cell's control, and Space presses it; the rest is Table's.
An override passes the keys it does not take to `super.onKeyDown(e)`.

## datapath
The grid's own rows: its `items`, wrapped so each record becomes a row. Set by the grid; not
yours to point elsewhere.
*/

include [ "table.declare" ]

class DataGrid extends Table [
    accessibilityRole = grid,

    // the marker rows and headers guard on

    _isDataGrid: boolean = true,

    headerH: number = { provided("theme").gridHeaderHeight },

    // ── what it shows: the records, and the columns as records ─────────────

    items: array = null,
    columns: array = [],
    headingClass: string = "",

    // ── the column-model state — the value pattern ────────────────────────

    order: array = null,        // column ids, presented order (null = as written)
    widths: object = null,      // { id: width } overrides (null = as written)
    sortOn: string = "",        // the column id the sort names ("" = unsorted)
    sortDir: string = "",       // "asc" | "desc"

    // ── the delivery seams — defaults write the attributes; use-site overrides
    //    own the state (persisted layouts, app-derived sorts) ──────────────

    sortInput(on: string, dir: string) { sortOn = on; sortDir = dir },
    arrangeInput(ord: array) { order = ord },
    resizeInput(id: string, w: number) { widths = ({ ...(widths ?? ({})), [id]: w }) },

    headerClick(id: string) {
        this.sortInput(id, sortOn == id && sortDir == "asc" ? "desc" : "asc")
        },

    // a heading between rows is not a row a person picks

    selectable(m: object) -> boolean { return m == null || (m as any).heading == null },

    // ── the column records: `columns`, ordered and widthed by the state, with
    //    running x offsets — the one derived truth every header cell and grid
    //    cell positions from. The inputs ride in as arguments, so the deriving
    //    constraint tracks them ───────────────────────────────────────────

    _columnRecords(cols: array, ord: array, w: object) -> array {
        const list = []
        for (const c of (cols ?? [])) { if (c != null && c.id != null) list.push(c) }
        if (ord != null) {
            const rank = (c) => {
                const i = ord.indexOf("" + c.id)
                return i < 0 ? ord.length + list.indexOf(c) : i
                }
            list.sort((a, b) => rank(a) - rank(b))
            }
        const out = []
        let x = 0
        for (const c of list) {
            const id = "" + c.id
            const wd = w != null && typeof (w as any)[id] == "number" ? (w as any)[id] : (typeof c.width == "number" ? c.width : 120)
            // width 0 drops the column — the deliberate narrow degrade
            // (priority, not squish): a responsive `widths` state zeroes the
            // low-priority columns and the model omits them entirely
            if (wd <= 0) continue
            out.push({ id: id, title: "" + (c.title ?? id), width: wd, x: x,
                cell: c.cell != null ? "" + c.cell : "", edit: c.edit == true, i: out.length })
            x = x + wd
            }
        return out
        },

    _currentOrder() -> array { return this.colData.value.cols.map((r) => r.id) },

    // the column model as a dataset, which the header and every row read

    colData: Dataset [ contents = { { cols: classroot._columnRecords(classroot.columns, classroot.order, classroot.widths) } } ],

    // the rows: one per item, replicated over the items as a dataset that holds
    // them (so a row's write lands in their source); a heading record is a
    // heading row

    // the items, as the dataset the rows replicate over; it holds the records it is
    // given, so a write through a row lands in their source

    _rowsData: Dataset [ contents = { { rows: classroot.items ?? [] } } ],
    datapath = { this._rowsData.value },

    TableRow [ datapath = :rows[], virtualize = true,
        classFor = { :heading != null ? DataGridHeadingRow : DataGridRow } ],

    // reorder-by-drag commits on release, never live: swapping under the
    // pointer would re-derive the spans mid-drag and flicker. While a header
    // drags, `_dragCol`/`_dropAt` drive the insertion bar (the vertical divider
    // at the would-be slot), and the order commits at the drop.

    _dragCol: string = "",
    _dropAt: number = -1,

    _dropIndexFor(cx: number) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        for (let i = 0; i < cols.length; i = i + 1) {
            if (cx < cols[i].x + cols[i].width / 2) return i
            }
        return cols.length
        },

    // `at` rides in as an argument so the insertion bar's constraint tracks
    // it, as `_columnRecords` takes its state

    _dropBarX(at: number) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        if (at < 0) return -10
        if (at >= cols.length) {
            const last = cols[cols.length - 1]
            return last != null ? last.x + last.width : 0
            }
        return cols[at].x
        },

    _commitDrop(id: string) {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const from = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        const at = _dropAt
        _dragCol = ""
        _dropAt = -1
        if (from < 0 || at < 0) return
        const to = at > from ? at - 1 : at
        if (to == from) return
        const ord = cols.map((r) => r.id)
        ord.splice(from, 1)
        ord.splice(to, 0, id)
        this.arrangeInput(ord)
        },

    // ── the active cell: the keyboard position in two dimensions — the active
    //    row is Table's, the column is this. Left/Right move the column; Enter,
    //    F2 or a typed character enters the cell; inside it, Tab walks the row's
    //    cells and Esc comes back (planes.md §13.6.2) ──────────────────────

    activeCol: number = 0,

    // the keyboard position may be in the header row: Up from the first row goes
    // there, Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the
    // column, Down comes back to the rows

    inHeader: boolean = false,

    _firstRow() -> number {
        const n = this.itemCount()
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) { if (this.selectable(this.itemAt(i))) return i }
        return -1
        },

    _activeCell() -> DataGridCell? {
        const inst = this.children.find((c) => (c as DataGridRow)._isGridRow === true && (c as DataGridRow).tableIndex() == activeIndex)
        if (inst == null) return null
        return (inst as DataGridRow).cellAt(activeCol)
        },

    _enterCell(cell: DataGridCell, typed: string) {
        const ed = cell._editor()
        if (ed != null) {
            if (typed == "" || typed == " ") { Focus.focus(ed); return }
            // a typed character starts the edit with it: after this keypress,
            // so the key is not typed a second time into the field it moved to
            afterDelay(0, () => {
                Focus.focus(ed)                     // first: the edit records the value it began from
                cell._writeText(typed)
                afterSettle(() => { ed.select("end") })
                })
            return
            }
        const ctl = cell._firstControl()
        if (ctl == null) return
        if (typed == " ") (ctl as Control).press()
        else Focus.focus(ctl)
        },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        const n = this.colData.value.cols.length
        const plain = !e.meta && !e.ctrl && !e.alt
        if (e.key == "ArrowRight" && plain) { activeCol = Math.min(n - 1, activeCol + 1); return }
        if (e.key == "ArrowLeft" && plain) { activeCol = Math.max(0, activeCol - 1); return }
        if (inHeader) {
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" && plain) inHeader = false
            else if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") && plain && !e.repeat) {
                const c = this.colData.value.cols[activeCol]
                if (c != null) this.headerClick("" + c.id)
                }
            return
            }
        if (e.key == "ArrowUp" && plain && activeIndex <= this._firstRow()) { inHeader = true; return }
        const cell = this._activeCell()
        if (cell != null && plain && !e.repeat) {
            const kind = cell._kind()
            if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == "F2") && kind != "text") { this._enterCell(cell, ""); return }
            if (e.key == " " && kind == "own") { this._enterCell(cell, " "); return }
            if (kind == "edit" && e.key.length == 1 && e.key != " ") { this._enterCell(cell, e.key); return }
            }
        super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    // vertical arrows from an edit: the edit moves to the same column one row
    // up/down (the spreadsheet expectation), passing over headings — the
    // destination scrolls into existence and its field takes focus.
    //
    // The focus waits for the next frame (afterDelay 0) rather than an afterSettle
    // step because showRow scrolls the platform scroller, and that scroll
    // lands as an event after this settle: the windowed rows re-link on it,
    // and a field whose row element moves loses the focus it was just
    // given. By the next frame the rows have re-seated.

    _editStep(row: View, col: number, d: number) {
        const n = this.itemCount()
        let i = (row as TableRow).tableIndex() + d
        while (i >= 0 && i < n && !this.selectable(this.itemAt(i))) i = i + d
        if (i < 0 || i >= n) return
        this.showRow(i)
        afterDelay(0, () => {
            const inst = this.children.find((c) => (c as DataGridRow)._isGridRow === true && (c as DataGridRow).tableIndex() == i)
            if (inst == null) return
            const cell = (inst as DataGridRow).cellAt(col)
            const ed = cell != null ? cell._editor() : null
            if (ed != null) Focus.focus(ed)
            })
        },

    _prevColId(id: string) -> string {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const i = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        return i > 0 ? cols[i - 1].id : ""
        },

    _colWidthOf(id: string) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const r = cols.find((c) => c.id == id)
        return r != null ? r.width : 0
        },

    // ── the header: chrome above the scroll region. The grid's box is the
    //    rows' scroll region exactly — the scrollbar spans rows only, no
    //    row ever slides under the header. A scroller clips at its box, so
    //    the header paints in the grid's parent (paintsIn — planes.md §3.1)
    //    and positions in the parent's frame; it stays the grid's member, so
    //    the column model sees nothing move, and the hit walk finds it among
    //    the parent's paint guests. ignoreLayout keeps full-mode stacking and
    //    the kernel's leading anchor off it — rows start at content 0 ────────

    hdr: View [ height = { classroot.headerH },
        paintsIn = { classroot.parent },
        x = { classroot.x },
        y = { classroot.y - classroot.headerH },

        // painting elsewhere, it leaves the grid's display subtree, so the
        // grid's own visibility does not reach it through the platform — mirror it

        visible = { classroot.visible },
        width = { classroot.width },
        ignoreLayout = true,
        ignoreClip = true,
        fill = { provided("theme").surface },
        datapath = { classroot.colData.value },
        accessibilityRole = row,

        View [ datapath = :cols[], claim = x,
            colid: string = { "" + :id },

            // a column header: named by its title, saying how its column is sorted;
            // the keyboard's position when the grid's is in the header row

            accessibilityRole = columnHeader,
            accessibilitySort = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn != colid ? "none" : (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "descending" : "ascending" },
            _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
            activeHere: boolean = { (classroot as DataGrid).focused && (classroot as DataGrid).inHeader && (classroot as DataGrid).activeCol == _ci },
            trackChanges = ["activeHere"],
            onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
                const g = classroot as DataGrid
                if (activeHere) g._activeView = this
                else if (g._activeView == this) g._activeView = null
                },
            x = { dragging && moved ? dragX : :x },
            width = { :width },
            height = 100%,

            // dragging lifts the header: an opaque ghost above its
            // neighbors (raise + surface fill + edge), tracking the pointer;
            // the grid's insertion bar shows the would-be slot; the order
            // commits at release (drop-commit — no mid-drag re-derives)

            dragging: boolean = false,
            moved: boolean = false,
            dragX: number = 0,
            x0: number = 0,
            p0: number = 0,

            // the lifted ghost is unmistakably in hand: an accent-edged,
            // shadowed, rounded card floating over its neighbors

            fill = { dragging && moved ? provided("theme").surface : null },
            stroke = { dragging && moved ? stroke(1.5, provided("theme").accent) : activeHere ? stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
            shadow = { dragging && moved ? (provided("theme").dragShadow) : null },
            cornerRadius = { dragging && moved ? 6 : activeHere ? provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius : 0 },
            opacity = { dragging && moved ? 0.95 : 1 },

            onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
                if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                dragging = true
                moved = false
                x0 = this.x
                dragX = this.x
                p0 = app.pointerX
                },
            onPointerMove() {
                if (!dragging) return
                const dx = app.pointerX - p0
                if (!moved && Math.abs(dx) > 3) { moved = true; this.raise() }
                dragX = x0 + dx
                if (moved) {
                    (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol = colid
                    ;(classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt = (classroot as DataGrid)._dropIndexFor(dragX + :width / 2)
                    }
                },
            onPointerUp() {
                if (!dragging) return
                dragging = false
                if (moved) (classroot as DataGrid)._commitDrop(colid)
                // an unmoved press is the click — the sort delivery
                else (classroot as DataGrid).headerClick(colid)
                },

            lbl: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridHeaderFontSize }, fontWeight = semibold, wrap = false,
                textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                text = { "" + :title } ],

            // The sort direction, drawn: one chevron class, turned by its
            // `dir`, so a sort header agrees with every other disclosure mark
            // in the chrome.

            sortMark: ChevronIcon [ y = center, iconSize = 11,
                x = { parent.width - this.width - 14 },
                visible = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn == parent.colid },
                ink = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                dir = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "down" : "up" } ],

            // the resize grip: the left-edge sliver, resizing the previous
            // column — the boundary region belongs to this cell (later
            // siblings hit first), so the grip must live here to be
            // reachable; absent on the first column. Plain widths delivered
            // through resizeInput — nothing measures anything.

            grip: View [ width = 10, height = 100%,
                x = -5,
                visible = { (:x ?? 0) > 0 },
                cursor = "col-resize", claim = x,
                resizing: boolean = false,
                pid: string = "",
                w0: number = 0,
                g0: number = 0,
                onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
                    if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                    pid = (classroot as DataGrid)._prevColId(this.parent.colid)
                    if (pid == "") return
                    w0 = (classroot as DataGrid)._colWidthOf(pid)
                    g0 = app.pointerX
                    resizing = true
                    },
                onPointerMove() {
                    if (!resizing) return
                    (classroot as DataGrid).resizeInput(pid, Math.max(40, w0 + app.pointerX - g0))
                    },
                onPointerUp() { resizing = false }
                ],

            hair: View [ width = 1, height = { parent.height - 10 }, y = 5,
                x = { parent.width - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
            ],

        // the insertion bar — where the dragged column would land

        drop: View [ width = 2, y = 2, height = { parent.height - 4 },
            visible = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol != "" },
            x = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dropBarX((classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt) - 1 },
            fill = { provided("theme").accent } ],

        // the hairline under the header. It stays a child view rather than
        // the header's own bottom stroke: a per-side stroke is a bare list,
        // and its colour here is a theme read.

        rule: View [ x = 0, width = 100%, height = 1,
            y = { parent.height - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
        ]
    ]


// ── The rows ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*
# DataGridRow

**Internal.** A row of a `DataGrid`, one per item, holding one `DataGridCell` per column
placed from the column model.

## rec
The row's record, as a derived attribute, so its cells follow the record's changes.

## _grid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` it belongs to.

## cellAt()
The cell in a column, by its place in the column model.

## _reportsActive
**Internal.** Off: in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, and the cell reports itself, not the row.

## _isGridRow
**Internal.** The marker identifying a grid row — not API.

## height
From the theme's `gridRowHeight` (28 by default); the header row is `gridHeaderHeight` (30).
*/

class DataGridRow extends TableRow [
    accessibilityRole = row,
    _isGridRow: boolean = true,
    _reportsActive = false,          // in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, which reports itself

    height = { provided("theme").gridRowHeight },

    rec: object = { this.datapath != null ? :@ : null },
    _grid: DataGrid? = { (this.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? this.parent as DataGrid : null },

    // one cell per column: its place and what it draws, and the row's record
    // in a list of its own — the record itself, never a copy, so what a cell
    // writes lands in the record

    // the row's cells as a dataset: for each column, its place and what it draws, with
    // the row's record — the record itself, so a cell's write lands in it

    _cellData: Dataset [ contents = { { cells: classroot._grid == null ? [] : classroot._grid.colData.value.cols.map((c) =>
        ({ id: c.id, title: c.title, x: c.x, width: c.width, cell: c.cell, edit: c.edit, i: c.i, row: [classroot.rec] })) } } ],

    cellAt(col: number) -> DataGridCell? {
        return (this.cells.children.find((c) => (c as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true && (c as DataGridCell)._ci == col) ?? null) as DataGridCell | null
        },

    cells: View [ x = 0, y = 0, width = 100%, height = 100%,
        datapath = { classroot._cellData.value },
        DataGridCell [ datapath = :cells[] ]
        ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridHeadingRow

**Internal.** The row a `DataGrid` makes for a record with a `heading` field, drawing the
grid's `headingClass` or `DataGridHeading` across the full width.

## rec
The heading record.

## _grid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` it belongs to.

## _isGridHeading
**Internal.** The marker identifying a heading row — not API.

## height
The theme's `gridRowHeight`, the same as an ordinary row.

## _kind
**Internal.** The class the heading row draws: the grid's `headingClass`, else
`DataGridHeading`.
*/

class DataGridHeadingRow extends TableRow [
    accessibilityRole = row,
    _isGridHeading: boolean = true,
    _reportsActive = false,
    focusable = false,
    height = { provided("theme").gridRowHeight },
    rec: object = { this.datapath != null ? :@ : null },
    _grid: DataGrid? = { (this.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? this.parent as DataGrid : null },
    _kind: string = { this._grid != null && this._grid.headingClass != "" ? this._grid.headingClass : "DataGridHeading" },

    // the heading record in a list of its own, for the class that draws it

    _headData: Dataset [ contents = { { one: [classroot.rec] } } ],
    View [ width = 100%, height = 100%, datapath = { classroot._headData.value },
        kind: string = { classroot._kind },
        View [ datapath = :one[], width = 100%, height = 100%,
            classFor = { parent.kind == "DataGridHeading" ? DataGridHeading : parent.kind } ]
        ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridHeading

**Internal.** The grid's own heading: the record's `heading` text, semibold, in the
theme's muted ink.
*/

class DataGridHeading [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, fontWeight = semibold, wrap = false,
        textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted }, text = { "" + (:heading ?? "") } ]
    ]


// ── The cells ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*
# DataGridCell

One cell of a `DataGrid` row: placed and sized from its column, with the row's record as the
data of what it draws — the column's `cell` class, an edit field for an `edit` column, or the
field as text. The grid makes it; a program writes the class it draws.

## _kind()
**Internal.** What the cell holds: `"own"` (the column's class), `"edit"` (a text field) or `"text"`.

## _drawn()
**Internal.** What the cell draws, as a view: its one child view.

## _editor()
**Internal.** The text field of an `edit` cell, or null.

## _firstControl()
**Internal.** The first focusable view inside the cell's own class, or null.

## _writeText()
**Internal.** Writes a text value into the cell's field in its row's record.

## _dataGrid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` its row belongs to.

## activeHere
Whether it is the grid's active cell while the grid has focus.

## _takeActive()
**Internal.** Makes this the grid's active cell, as its edit field does when it takes the focus.

## _isGridCell
**Internal.** The marker identifying a grid cell — not API.

## _ci
**Internal.** Its column's place in the column model — what the grid's `activeCol` names.

## _fld
**Internal.** The field its column shows.

## _cls
**Internal.** The class it draws.

## _reportActive()
**Internal.** The cell that becomes the grid's active cell records itself as the grid's
`_activeView`, which the grid names to a screen reader; one that stops being it takes itself
back.

## x
Its column's offset in the row.

## width
Its column's width.

## stroke
The outline that marks the active cell.

## cornerRadius
From the theme's `gridCellCornerRadius` (3 by default): the active cell's outline.

## _row
**Internal.** The `DataGridRow` this cell is drawn in, or null outside one.

## gridFocused
Whether the grid holds keyboard focus in its body (not its header), so a cell can show
where the keyboard is.
*/

class DataGridCell [ x = { :x }, width = { :width }, height = 100%, clip = true,
    accessibilityRole = cell,
    _isGridCell: boolean = true,
    _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
    _fld: string = { "" + :id },
    _cls: string = { :edit == true ? "DataGridEdit" : ("" + (:cell ?? "")) != "" ? "" + :cell : "DataGridText" },

    _kind() -> string { return this._cls == "DataGridEdit" ? "edit" : this._cls == "DataGridText" ? "text" : "own" },

    // what it draws, as a view: its one child view

    _drawn() -> View? { return this.childViews.length > 0 ? this.childViews[0] : null },

    _editor() -> TextInput? {
        const d = this._drawn()
        return d != null && (d as DataGridEdit)._isGridEdit === true ? (d as DataGridEdit).ed : null
        },

    _firstControl() -> View? {
        const d = this._drawn()
        if (d == null) return null
        const walk = (v: View) => {
            for (const c of v.childViews) {
                if (c.focusable == true && c.visible) return c
                const deeper = walk(c)
                if (deeper != null) return deeper
                }
            return null
            }
        return walk(d)
        },

    _writeText(v: string) {
        const d = this._drawn()
        if (d != null && (d as DataGridEdit)._isGridEdit === true) (d as DataGridEdit).write(v)
        },

    // the active cell — the grid's keyboard position — is outlined while the
    // grid has focus

    _row: DataGridRow? = { (this.parent?.parent as DataGridRow)?._isGridRow === true ? this.parent.parent as DataGridRow : null },
    _dataGrid: DataGrid? = { this._row != null ? this._row._grid : null },
    gridFocused: boolean = { this._dataGrid != null && this._dataGrid.focused && !this._dataGrid.inHeader },
    activeHere: boolean = { this.gridFocused && this._row?.active == true && this._dataGrid?.activeCol == this._ci },
    stroke = { this.activeHere ? stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
    cornerRadius = { provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius },

    // the active cell says so to its grid, which names it to a screen reader —
    // heard by this member, so a cell class's own `trackChanges` and `onChange`
    // are its own

    _watch: Node [
        on: boolean = { classroot.activeHere },
        trackChanges = ["on"],
        onChange() { classroot._reportActive() }
        ],
    _reportActive() {
        const g = this._dataGrid
        if (g == null) return
        if (this.activeHere) g._activeView = this
        else if (g._activeView == this) g._activeView = null
        },

    // a control in the cell taking focus — by a press or by Tab inside the row —
    // makes this the active cell

    _takeActive() {
        const g = this._dataGrid
        const r = this._row
        if (g == null || r == null) return
        g.activeCol = this._ci
        const i = r.tableIndex()
        g.activeIndex = i
        g.active = g.itemAt(i)
        },

    // what it draws: one view over the row's record, of the class its column
    // names

    View [ datapath = :row[], width = 100%, height = 100%,
        classFor = { parent._cls == "DataGridEdit" ? DataGridEdit : parent._cls == "DataGridText" ? DataGridText : parent._cls } ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridText

**Internal.** What a `DataGrid` column with no `cell` draws: its field, as text.

## field
The field shown — its column's id.
*/

class DataGridText [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    field: string = { (parent as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true ? (parent as DataGridCell)._fld : "" },
    t: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, wrap = false,
        text = { "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "") } ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridEdit

**Internal.** What an `edit: true` column of a `DataGrid` draws: a text field writing its
field into the row's record as you type.

## field
The field edited — its column's id.

## write()
Writes a text value into the field.

## _before
**Internal.** The text when the edit began, which Esc puts back.

## _isGridEdit
**Internal.** The marker identifying an edit cell's field — not API.
*/

class DataGridEdit [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    _isGridEdit: boolean = true,
    field: string = { (parent as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true ? (parent as DataGridCell)._fld : "" },
    _before: string = "",
    write(v: string) { :@[(field)] = v },

    ed: TextInput [ x = 4, y = 3, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize },
        accessibilityLabel = { (classroot.parent as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true ? "" + (classroot.parent as any).title : "" },
        width = { parent.width - 8 },
        height = { parent.height - 6 },
        initial = { "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "") },
        onFocus() {
            classroot._before = "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "")
            const cell = classroot.parent as DataGridCell
            if (cell._isGridCell === true) cell._takeActive()
            },
        onInput(v: string) { classroot.write(v) },
        onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
            const cell = classroot.parent as DataGridCell
            if (cell._isGridCell !== true || cell._row == null || cell._dataGrid == null) return
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" || e.key == "Enter") cell._dataGrid._editStep(cell._row, cell._ci, 1)
            else if (e.key == "ArrowUp") cell._dataGrid._editStep(cell._row, cell._ci, -1)
            else if (e.key == "Escape") classroot.write(classroot._before)
            }
        ]
    ]
